+<section>
+<h2>1. An ADC Code Is a Ratio, Not a Voltage</h2>
+
+<p>The single most important idea in this lesson: <strong>the number an ADC returns has no voltage unit attached to it.</strong> A 12-bit ADC answers exactly one question — <em>"what fraction of the reference span is the input sitting at?"</em> — quantised into 4096 steps.</p>
+
+<p>The reference voltage is the <strong>unit of measure</strong>. Reading code <code>2047</code> tells you the input is at roughly half the reference — and nothing more. Half of <em>what</em> depends entirely on which reference was selected when the conversion ran:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Code returned</th><th>Selected reference (VR+)</th><th>Actual input voltage</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>2047</code></td><td>1.2 V (internal VREF)</td><td>≈ 0.60 V</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>2047</code></td><td>2.5 V (internal VREF2.5)</td><td>≈ 1.25 V</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>2047</code></td><td>3.3 V (AVCC)</td><td>≈ 1.65 V</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>Same code, three different physical voltages. Conversely, the <strong>same input voltage produces different codes under different references</strong>:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Input</th><th>Reference (VR+)</th><th>Code (12-bit)</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>1.2 V</td><td>1.2 V (VREF)</td><td><code>4095</code></td><td>Full scale — input equals the reference</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>1.2 V</td><td>2.0 V (VREF2.0)</td><td><code>2457</code></td><td>60% of span</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>1.2 V</td><td>2.5 V (VREF2.5)</td><td><code>1965</code></td><td>48% of span</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>1.2 V</td><td>3.3 V (AVCC)</td><td><code>1489</code></td><td>≈36% of span</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 The mental model</div>
+  Think of the reference as a ruler. The ADC reports "the input reaches the 1489th tick mark of my 4096-tick ruler." If you swap in a shorter ruler (a smaller reference), the same input reaches a <em>higher</em> tick mark. The tick number is meaningless until you know which ruler was used.
+</div>
+</section>
