+<div class="term" id="reference-voltage">
+  <h2>Reference voltage (V<sub>R+</sub> / V<sub>R−</sub>)</h2>
+  <div class="pron">the conversion's unit of measure</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The voltage pair an ADC compares its input against: V<sub>R+</sub> maps to the full-scale code, V<sub>R−</sub> maps to code 0. Every output code is a fraction of the span between them — the reference literally defines what one "unit" of the result means. Change the reference and the same physical input produces a different code.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>In <code>silmulator/devices/msp430.py</code>, <code>_adc_reference_voltage()</code> resolves V<sub>R+</sub> at conversion time — <code>clock.vcc</code> for AVCC, <code>_veref_v</code> for VEREF, or an <code>ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V</code> lookup for REF_A's outputs. V<sub>R−</sub> is fixed at AVSS (0 V) in this model. Before commit 58b7e2d, this function didn't exist and V<sub>R+</sub> was a hardcoded <code>3.3</code>.</dd>
