+    <dd>A measurement arrangement where the sensor's output and the ADC's reference derive from the same source, so the conversion measures the sensor's <em>fraction</em> and the source's absolute value cancels out of the transfer function: N = FS × (k·AVCC)/AVCC = FS × k.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>Selecting <code>ADC:REFERENCE AVCC</code> makes <code>_adc_reference_voltage()</code> return <code>self.clock.vcc</code> live. A divider-style sensor modelled by injecting <code>k × vcc</code> reads the same code before and after <code>CPU:VCC 3.0</code> — while an absolute injection like the fixed 1.2 V shifts from 1489 to 1638, which is how a SIL test tells the two arrangements apart.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"The potentiometer is powered from the rail, so keep the conversion ratiometric — referencing it to VREF2.5 would re-introduce the supply drift you're trying to cancel."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: absolute measurement</dt>
+    <dd>Absolute measurement answers "how many volts?" and needs a supply-independent reference (REF_A or VeREF+). Ratiometric measurement answers "what fraction of the excitation?" and <em>wants</em> the supply as the reference. Choosing the wrong mode for the sensor type is a real firmware bug class the reference-aware model can now surface.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<hr style="border:none;border-top:1px solid var(--border);margin:2.5rem 0">
+<p style="color:var(--dim);font-size:.83rem">
