+// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+const BANK = [
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: transfer ────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'t1',ch:'transfer',type:'w',
+ q:'Write out the ADC12_B transfer function from SLAU367P §34.2.1 (Eq. 13) and explain what each of the four quantities means. What does the equation simplify to in Silmulator\'s model, and why?',
+ c:[{l:'NADC = FS × (Vin − VR−)/(VR+ − VR−)',t:['4095','fs ×','(vin','vr−','vr-','vr+']},
+    {l:'VR+ = positive reference (full-scale voltage)',t:['vr+','positive reference','full scale','full-scale']},
+    {l:'VR− = negative reference (code-0 voltage)',t:['vr−','vr-','negative reference','code 0','zero']},
+    {l:'VR− fixed at AVSS (0 V) → N = FS × Vin/VR+',t:['avss','0 v','fixed','simplif','vin / vr','vin/vr']}],
+ m:`NADC = FS × (Vin − VR−) / (VR+ − VR−), where FS = 2^resolution − 1 (4095 in 12-bit mode).
+- Vin: the analog input voltage on the selected channel.
+- VR+: the positive reference — the voltage that produces the full-scale code. Selected by ADC12VRSEL.
+- VR−: the negative reference — the voltage that produces code 0.
+- FS: the full-scale code (4095 / 1023 / 255 for 12/10/8-bit ADC12RES modes).
+In Silmulator, VR− is fixed at AVSS (0 V) — the _adc_reference_voltage() docstring says the model only exposes which source drives VR+, not the VR− half of ADC12VRSEL's combinations. So the equation simplifies to N = FS × Vin / VR+, implemented as int(voltage / full_scale_v * max_code), clamped to [0, max_code].`
+},
+
+{id:'t2',ch:'transfer',type:'mc',
+ q:'ADC:REFERENCE AVCC is selected (CPU:VCC at its 3.3-V default) and 1.2 V is injected on channel 0. What does ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW? return in 12-bit mode?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># msp430_faults.py — nominal profile</span>
+code = int(voltage / full_scale_v * max_code)
+<span class="kw">return</span> max(<span class="num">0</span>, min(max_code, code))`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'1489',c:true, w:'Correct. int(1.2 / 3.3 × 4095) = int(1489.09) = 1489 — about 36% of the reference span.'},
+  {t:'4095',c:false,w:'4095 would require Vin ≥ VR+. That happens with the same 1.2-V input only when the 1.2-V VREF is the selected reference.'},
+  {t:'1490',c:false,w:'int() truncates: 1489.09 → 1489. There is no rounding to nearest in adc_code().'},
+  {t:'1229',c:false,w:'1229 ≈ int(1.2/4.0×4095) — wrong reference voltage. AVCC defaults to 3.3 V (clock.vcc).'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'t3',ch:'transfer',type:'mc',
+ q:'Same 1.2-V injection on channel 0, but now the test first sends ADC:REFERENCE VREF. What does ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW? return?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'4095 — the input equals the 1.2-V reference, so it reads full scale',c:true, w:'Correct. Bare VREF maps to REFVSEL\'s reset default (00b = 1.2 V) via ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V. N = 4095 × 1.2/1.2 = 4095. This is exactly test_raw_respects_selected_reference and the adc_ignores_reference audit probe.'},
+  {t:'1489 — the reference selection is stored but conversions always use 3.3 V',c:false,w:'That was the pre-58b7e2d bug. The fix makes _adc_ch_raw call _adc_reference_voltage(), so VREF (1.2 V) is actually used.'},
+  {t:'2047 — VREF means half of AVCC',c:false,w:'VREF is REF_A\'s internal 1.2-V output (REFVSEL reset value), not a fraction of the supply.'},
+  {t:'SCPI error -224 — bare VREF is ambiguous and must be VREF1.2/2.0/2.5',c:false,w:'Bare VREF is valid: it maps to the REFVSEL reset default of 1.2 V in ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'t4',ch:'transfer',type:'w',
+ q:'A 0.6-V signal is injected on channel 5. Compute the 12-bit RAW code under each of these three references: VREF1.2, VREF2.5, and AVCC (3.3 V). Show your arithmetic, including the truncation step.',
+ c:[{l:'VREF1.2 → 2047 (0.6/1.2×4095 = 2047.5, truncated)',t:['2047']},
+    {l:'VREF2.5 → 982 (0.6/2.5×4095 = 982.8)',t:['982']},
+    {l:'AVCC 3.3 → 744 (0.6/3.3×4095 = 744.5)',t:['744']},
+    {l:'int() truncates toward zero',t:['truncat','int(','toward zero','floor']}],
+ m:`All use N = int(0.6 / VR+ × 4095):
+- VREF1.2: 0.6/1.2 = 0.5 → 0.5 × 4095 = 2047.5 → int() truncates → 2047
+- VREF2.5: 0.6/2.5 = 0.24 → 0.24 × 4095 = 982.8 → 982
+- AVCC 3.3: 0.6/3.3 ≈ 0.1818 → × 4095 = 744.54 → 744
+Same physical voltage, three different codes — the code is a fraction of the selected reference, not a voltage. Note int() truncates toward zero (2047.5 → 2047, never 2048).`
+},
+
+{id:'t5',ch:'transfer',type:'mc',
+ q:'Firmware reads code 2047 from a 12-bit conversion. Which statement is the ONLY safe conclusion?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The input is at roughly half of whatever reference span was selected for the conversion',c:true, w:'Correct. 2047/4095 ≈ 0.5 — a ratio. It could be ~0.6 V (VREF), ~1.25 V (VREF2.5), or ~1.65 V (AVCC at 3.3). Without knowing VR+, the code carries no voltage information.'},
+  {t:'The input voltage is approximately 1.65 V',c:false,w:'Only true if VR+ happened to be 3.3 V. Under VREF (1.2 V) the same code means ~0.6 V. The code alone does not determine the voltage.'},
+  {t:'The ADC is misconfigured, because 2047 is not a power of two',c:false,w:'Codes can be any integer in [0, 4095]. There is nothing special or wrong about 2047.'},
+  {t:'The input exceeds the reference and the result has clipped',c:false,w:'Clipping pegs the code at 4095 (full scale), not 2047.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: refs ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'r1',ch:'refs',type:'w',
+ q:'List every token ADC:REFERENCE accepts after commit 58b7e2d, the VR+ voltage each one selects, and the physical source it models.',
+ c:[{l:'AVCC → clock.vcc live (supply rail)',t:['avcc','clock.vcc','cpu:vcc','supply','3.3']},
+    {l:'VREF / VREF1.2 → 1.2 V (REFVSEL reset default)',t:['vref','1.2','reset default','00b']},
+    {l:'VREF2.0 → 2.0 V; VREF2.5 → 2.5 V (REF_A outputs)',t:['2.0','2.5','ref_a','internal']},
+    {l:'VEREF → _veref_v, external VeREF+ pin, settable via 2nd param',t:['veref','external','pin','_veref_v','second param','2.1']}],
+ m:`VALID_ADC_REFERENCES = frozenset({"AVCC", "VEREF"} | set(ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V)) — six tokens:
+- AVCC → whatever CPU:VCC currently is (read live from clock.vcc; default 3.3 V). Models the analog supply rail.
+- VREF → 1.2 V. Bare VREF maps to REFVSEL's reset value (00b = 1.2 V) in REF_A.
+- VREF1.2 → 1.2 V, VREF2.0 → 2.0 V, VREF2.5 → 2.5 V. REF_A's three internal bandgap-derived outputs (SLAU367P §33.3.1).
+- VEREF → self._veref_v (default 1.2 V; reset by *RST). Models the external VeREF+ pin; the board-supplied voltage is settable with a second parameter, e.g. ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.1.
+Anything else has no ADC12VRSEL encoding → ScpiError -224.`
+},
+
+{id:'r2',ch:'refs',type:'mc',
+ q:'A test script sends ADC:REFERENCE BANANA. Per the fixed code and probe_adc_reference_unvalidated, what is the device state afterwards?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># scripts/audit_msp430.py — after 58b7e2d</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_adc_reference_unvalidated</span>(dev):
+    <span class="str">"""Reference is validated against the fixed ADC12VRSEL/REF_A set."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"ADC:REFERENCE BANANA"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">return</span> _errors(dev) == <span class="num">1</span> <span class="kw">and</span> dev.adc[<span class="num">0</span>].reference == <span class="str">"AVCC"</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'One SCPI error (-224) is queued and the reference is still the AVCC default — the bad value is never stored',c:true, w:'Correct. _adc_ref raises ScpiError(-224, "Illegal ADC reference: BANANA") BEFORE the loop that assigns ch.reference, so channel state is untouched. The probe asserts exactly this: errors == 1 and reference == "AVCC".'},
+  {t:'The reference is set to BANANA and conversions fall back to 3.3 V',c:false,w:'That was the OLD behaviour (any string accepted, never used). The validation now rejects it before storage.'},
+  {t:'Error -224 is queued but the reference is also cleared to VREF as a safe default',c:false,w:'Rejection leaves prior state unchanged — it does not reset to some other default. The probe checks reference == "AVCC" because AVCC was the state before the bad command.'},
+  {t:'The command is silently ignored with no error',c:false,w:'SCPI devices report bad parameters via the error queue. The probe requires _errors(dev) == 1.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'r3',ch:'refs',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why does the simulator reject ADC:REFERENCE 1.8 rather than supporting arbitrary reference voltages?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The real ADC12VRSEL register field has a finite set of encodings — there is no silicon path that produces an arbitrary 1.8-V reference',c:true, w:'Correct. VR+ must come from a physical source: AVCC, one of REF_A\'s three bandgap outputs, or the external VeREF+ pin. A free-form voltage would let SIL tests bless configurations no hardware can express — the simulator must mirror the register\'s value set.'},
+  {t:'Floating-point parameters are not supported by the SCPI parser',c:false,w:'The parser handles floats fine — ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.1 takes one. The rejection is about hardware fidelity, not parsing.'},
+  {t:'1.8 V would exceed the maximum safe reference for the FR5994',c:false,w:'2.5-V and 3.3-V references are valid, so 1.8 V is not out of range. It simply corresponds to no ADC12VRSEL encoding.'},
+  {t:'It is a temporary limitation until REF_A trimming is modelled',c:false,w:'It is deliberate and permanent: the accepted set mirrors the hardware\'s selectable sources. (What IS still missing from REF_A is REFCTL0 semantics — REFON/REFOUT bits etc.)'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'r4',ch:'refs',type:'w',
+ q:'Explain the two-parameter form ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.1 — what each parameter does, what happens if the second parameter is "notanumber", and what *RST does to this state.',
+ c:[{l:'param 1 selects the external VeREF+ pin as VR+',t:['veref','external','pin','vr+','select']},
+    {l:'param 2 sets the board-supplied pin voltage (_veref_v = 2.1)',t:['_veref_v','2.1','board','voltage','second param','float']},
+    {l:'non-numeric → ScpiError -104 Data type error',t:['-104','data type','valueerror','notanumber']},
+    {l:'*RST resets _veref_v to 1.2',t:['*rst','reset','1.2']}],
+ m:`Parameter 1 (VEREF) selects the external VeREF+ pin as the VR+ source — _adc_reference_voltage() then returns self._veref_v at conversion time.
+Parameter 2 (2.1) sets the pin's voltage: the handler does self._veref_v = float(cmd.params[1]). This exists because VeREF+ is board-supplied (SLASE54D Table 7-1 — no defined reset state after BOR); the test declares what the board wires up.
+If the second parameter is "notanumber", float() raises ValueError and the handler converts it to ScpiError(-104, "Invalid VEREF voltage: notanumber") — a data-type error, distinct from -224 (illegal value). test_veref_voltage_param_must_be_numeric pins this: one queued error, no crash.
+*RST resets _veref_v to 1.2 along with the rest of the peripheral state, so the two-parameter form must be re-sent after any reset.`
+},
+
+{id:'r5',ch:'refs',type:'mc',
+ q:'What is the difference between SCPI errors -224 and -104 in the ADC:REFERENCE handler?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'-224 = token not in the permitted value set (e.g. BANANA); -104 = right token but the VEREF voltage parameter fails float() parsing',c:true, w:'Correct. -224 "Illegal parameter value": correct type, not an allowed value. -104 "Data type error": the second parameter must be numeric. Two different failure modes, two standard SCPI codes.'},
+  {t:'-224 is fatal and halts the device; -104 is a warning',c:false,w:'Neither halts anything — both queue an error and leave device state unchanged. Severity is identical; the meaning differs.'},
+  {t:'They are interchangeable; the codes were chosen arbitrarily',c:false,w:'They follow the SCPI standard\'s error classification: -1xx = command/data-type errors, -2xx = execution errors like illegal parameter values.'},
+  {t:'-104 is used for unknown reference tokens, -224 for bad voltages',c:false,w:'It is the reverse: -224 for the unknown token (BANANA), -104 for the malformed voltage (VEREF,notanumber).'},
+ ]
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: code ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'c1',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'What was the exact defect in _adc_ch_raw before commit 58b7e2d?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># BEFORE</span>
+<span class="kw">return</span> str(self.faults.adc_code(ac.value, <span class="num">3.3</span>, ac.resolution))
+<span class="cm"># AFTER</span>
+<span class="kw">return</span> str(self.faults.adc_code(ac.value, self.<span class="fn">_adc_reference_voltage</span>(), ac.resolution))`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The full-scale voltage argument was the hardcoded literal 3.3, so the stored ADC:REFERENCE selection never affected the conversion',c:true, w:'Correct. adc_code(value, full_scale_v, resolution) received 3.3 unconditionally. Selecting VREF (1.2/2.0/2.5 V) or changing AVCC via CPU:VCC never rescaled conversions — the audit finding on ADC:CHANNELn:RAW.'},
+  {t:'The resolution argument was hardcoded to 12 bits',c:false,w:'Resolution was already per-channel (ac.resolution) and compliant. The hardcoded value was the 3.3-V full scale.'},
+  {t:'adc_code() itself divided by the wrong constant internally',c:false,w:'adc_code() was correct — it divides by whatever full_scale_v it is given. The bug was in the caller, which always passed 3.3.'},
+  {t:'The handler returned the injected voltage instead of a code',c:false,w:'That describes ADC:CHANNELn:VALUE? (test introspection). RAW always returned a code — just one scaled by the wrong reference.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'c2',ch:'code',type:'w',
+ q:'Walk through _adc_reference_voltage() added in 58b7e2d: how is VR+ resolved for each reference kind, why does it read self.adc[0].reference, and what does the model deliberately NOT expose?',
+ snip:`<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_adc_reference_voltage</span>(self) -> float:
+    ref = self.adc[<span class="num">0</span>].reference
+    <span class="kw">if</span> ref == <span class="str">"AVCC"</span>:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> self.clock.vcc
+    <span class="kw">if</span> ref == <span class="str">"VEREF"</span>:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> self._veref_v
+    <span class="kw">return</span> ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V.get(ref, self.clock.vcc)`,
+ c:[{l:'AVCC → clock.vcc read live at conversion time',t:['clock.vcc','live','cpu:vcc','supply']},
+    {l:'VEREF → _veref_v (external pin state)',t:['_veref_v','veref','external','pin']},
+    {l:'else → ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V lookup (REF_A 1.2/2.0/2.5)',t:['adc_internal_ref_v','dict','lookup','1.2','2.0','2.5']},
+    {l:'adc[0] because _adc_ref sets ALL channels to one reference',t:['adc[0]','all channels','single','one reference','every channel']},
+    {l:'VR− half of ADC12VRSEL not exposed (fixed AVSS 0 V)',t:['vr−','vr-','avss','not expose','0 v','fixed']}],
+ m:`Three branches:
+1. AVCC → returns self.clock.vcc, read live at every conversion. CPU:VCC 3.0 immediately rescales the next RAW read — the supply IS the reference.
+2. VEREF → returns self._veref_v, the externally supplied VeREF+ pin voltage set via ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,<v>.
+3. Anything else → ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V.get(ref, self.clock.vcc): REF_A's internal outputs (VREF/VREF1.2 → 1.2, VREF2.0 → 2.0, VREF2.5 → 2.5), with clock.vcc as a defensive fallback.
+It reads self.adc[0].reference because the model keeps a single device-wide reference: the _adc_ref setter loops over all channels ("for ch in self.adc.values(): ch.reference = ref"), so channel 0 is representative of all of them.
+The docstring states the deliberate limitation: VR− is fixed at AVSS (0 V). Real ADC12VRSEL encodings also choose the VR− source; the simulator only models which source drives VR+.`
+},
+
+{id:'c3',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why was probe_adc_ignores_reference\'s expected value changed from int(1.2/3.3*4095) to 4095?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># BEFORE: return raw == int(1.2 / 3.3 * 4095)</span>
+<span class="cm"># AFTER:</span>
+dev.process(<span class="str">"ADC:REFERENCE VREF"</span>)
+dev.inject_adc(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1.2</span>)
+raw = int(dev.process(<span class="str">"ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW?"</span>))
+<span class="kw">return</span> raw == <span class="num">4095</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Audit probes assert the COMPLIANT behaviour: with the 1.2-V VREF selected, a 1.2-V input must read full scale — the old expectation documented the bug',c:true, w:'Correct. Probes encode what SLAU367P says should happen. Before the fix, the probe demonstrated the defect (1489 despite VREF); after it, the probe verifies Eq. 13 compliance (4095). The docstring flipped accordingly.'},
+  {t:'4095 is a rounding of 1489 to the nearest power-of-two boundary',c:false,w:'4095 is not derived from 1489 — it is full scale, the correct result of 4095 × 1.2/1.2 under the newly honoured 1.2-V reference.'},
+  {t:'The probe now tests a different channel with a different injection',c:false,w:'Channel 0 and the 1.2-V injection are unchanged. Only the expected code changed, because the reference now takes effect.'},
+  {t:'int(1.2/3.3*4095) stopped compiling after the refactor',c:false,w:'The expression is plain Python and always valid. It was replaced because it asserted the buggy 3.3-V scaling.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'c4',ch:'code',type:'w',
+ q:'Two tests in test_msp430_edge_cases.py had @pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True) removed by commit 58b7e2d. Name them, state what each asserts, and explain why strict xfail was the right marker before the fix.',
+ c:[{l:'test_reference_out_of_range_rejected — BANANA queues 1 error',t:['out_of_range','banana','rejected','errors(mcu) == 1','one error']},
+    {l:'test_raw_respects_selected_reference — VREF + 1.2 V → 4095',t:['respects_selected','4095','vref','1.2']},
+    {l:'strict xfail = documented known bug; XPASS fails the suite when fixed',t:['strict','xpass','known bug','documented','flip','fails if it passes']}],
+ m:`1. test_reference_out_of_range_rejected — sends ADC:REFERENCE BANANA and asserts errors(mcu) == 1, citing SLAU367P §34.2.4/§33: the reference comes from a fixed set, not free-form.
+2. test_raw_respects_selected_reference — selects VREF, injects 1.2 V, asserts ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW? == 4095 (Eq. 13: input equal to the reference is full scale).
+Before the fix, both were expected failures documenting audit findings. strict=True is the key: a strict xfail FAILS the suite if the test unexpectedly passes (XPASS). That means the test file doubles as a ledger of known non-compliances — the moment someone fixes the behaviour, the strict xfail forces them to remove the marker and promote the test to a real regression guard, which is exactly what 58b7e2d did. The commit also ADDED test_veref_voltage_param_must_be_numeric for the new -104 path.`
+},
+
+{id:'c5',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'Commit 58b7e2d added self._veref_v = 1.2 in two places in msp430.py. Which two, and why does the second matter?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'__init__ (initial state) and the *RST handler — device reset must restore every piece of peripheral state to its power-on value',c:true, w:'Correct. New mutable device state must be initialised AND reset. If *RST skipped _veref_v, a test that set VEREF,2.5 would leak that voltage into later tests that reset the device — a hidden inter-test dependency.'},
+  {t:'__init__ and _adc_ch_raw — the conversion path re-defaults it each read',c:false,w:'_adc_ch_raw only reads the reference voltage; it never writes _veref_v. Re-defaulting on read would make the two-parameter form useless.'},
+  {t:'_adc_ref and _adc_convert — both SCPI entry points',c:false,w:'_adc_ref assigns it only when a second parameter is given; _adc_convert never touches it. The two assignments of 1.2 are construction and *RST.'},
+  {t:'The ADCChannel dataclass and the FaultState profile',c:false,w:'_veref_v is device-level (one pin), not per-channel, and has nothing to do with fault profiles.'},
+ ]
+},
