+// ─── CHANNEL: clip ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'k1',ch:'clip',type:'mc',
+ q:'ADC:REFERENCE VREF2.0 is selected and 2.5 V is injected. What does a 12-bit RAW read return?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'4095 — the ideal 5118.75 is clamped to full scale',c:true, w:'Correct. 4095 × 2.5/2.0 = 5118.75, but adc_code() ends with max(0, min(max_code, code)). Inputs above VR+ saturate at full scale exactly like real hardware — no error, no wraparound.'},
+  {t:'5118 — codes above full scale are allowed for over-range inputs',c:false,w:'A 12-bit result register physically cannot hold 5118. The clamp caps it at 4095.'},
+  {t:'1022 — the result wraps modulo 4096',c:false,w:'No wraparound: the clamp is a saturation, min(max_code, code).'},
+  {t:'SCPI error -222 (data out of range) is queued',c:false,w:'Over-range analog inputs are not SCPI errors — the conversion silently pegs at full scale, as on silicon.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'k2',ch:'clip',type:'w',
+ q:'Define "LSB voltage" via the transfer function, compute it for VREF1.2 and AVCC 3.3 in 12-bit mode, and state the engineering trade-off in choosing a smaller reference.',
+ c:[{l:'LSB = (VR+ − VR−)/FS — the voltage of one code step',t:['(vr+','/ 4095','/4095','one code','step','fs']},
+    {l:'1.2 V → ≈293 µV/LSB',t:['293']},
+    {l:'3.3 V → ≈806 µV/LSB',t:['806','805']},
+    {l:'smaller ref = finer resolution but smaller range (clipping above VR+)',t:['finer','smaller range','clip','trade','resolution','headroom']}],
+ m:`One LSB is the input voltage change that moves the code by one step: LSB = (VR+ − VR−) / FS = VR+ / 4095 in this model.
+- VREF1.2: 1.2 / 4095 ≈ 293 µV per code.
+- AVCC 3.3: 3.3 / 4095 ≈ 806 µV per code.
+Trade-off: a smaller reference gives finer granularity (each code is worth fewer microvolts) but shrinks the measurable range — anything above VR+ clips to 4095. So you pick the smallest reference that still covers the signal with headroom. A 0–1 V signal against VREF1.2 uses ~83% of the code space; against AVCC it uses ~30%, wasting ~1.4 bits of effective resolution.`
+},
+
+{id:'k3',ch:'clip',type:'mc',
+ q:'After ADC:RESOLUTION 8, a 3.3-V injection is read with AVCC (3.3 V) selected. What code comes back, and what changed in the transfer function?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'255 — full scale; FS becomes 2⁸−1 while the reference is unchanged',c:true, w:'Correct (this is the existing edge-case test). Resolution sets FS = (1 << 8) − 1 = 255; the reference still sets the voltage span. int(3.3/3.3 × 255) = 255. Reference and resolution are the two independent knobs of N = FS × Vin/VR+.'},
+  {t:'4095 — resolution changes display formatting only',c:false,w:'Resolution changes max_code in adc_code(): (1 << resolution) − 1. In 8-bit mode the register can only hold 0–255.'},
+  {t:'255 — but only because 3.3 V clips against a now-smaller reference',c:false,w:'The reference did not change — it is still 3.3 V. 255 is exact full scale (Vin = VR+), not a clip above it.'},
+  {t:'SCPI error -224 — resolution must be set before the reference',c:false,w:'There is no ordering constraint between ADC:RESOLUTION and ADC:REFERENCE; both independently parameterise the next conversion.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'k4',ch:'clip',type:'w',
+ q:'A pressure sensor is a resistive divider powered from AVCC, outputting a fixed fraction k of the supply. Explain why converting it against AVCC (ratiometric measurement) is MORE accurate than using the precise VREF2.5 when the supply drifts — derive it from Eq. 13.',
+ c:[{l:'sensor output = k × AVCC',t:['k × avcc','k×avcc','fraction of the supply','fraction of avcc','k * avcc']},
+    {l:'against AVCC: N = FS × k·AVCC/AVCC = FS×k — supply cancels',t:['cancel','fs × k','fs×k','independent of','drops out']},
+    {l:'against VREF2.5: N = FS × k·AVCC/2.5 — code moves with supply drift',t:['2.5','moves','drift','tracks the supply','changes with']},
+    {l:'testable in Silmulator via live clock.vcc / CPU:VCC',t:['cpu:vcc','clock.vcc','live','silmulator','simulat']}],
+ m:`The sensor's output is Vin = k × AVCC (a divider scales its own supply).
+Against AVCC as reference: N = FS × (k × AVCC) / AVCC = FS × k. AVCC cancels — the code measures the sensor fraction k directly and is immune to supply drift. That is ratiometric measurement, and it is why AVCC exists as a reference choice.
+Against VREF2.5: N = FS × (k × AVCC) / 2.5. Now AVCC stays in the equation, so a supply sag from 3.3 to 3.0 V changes the code by ~9% even though the pressure (k) is unchanged — the "precise" absolute reference is the wrong tool for a ratiometric sensor.
+Silmulator can now exercise this because _adc_reference_voltage() reads clock.vcc live: set CPU:VCC 3.0 and an AVCC-referenced divider reading (if you re-inject k × 3.0) holds its code, while a VREF2.5-referenced one shifts.`
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: sil ─────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'s1',ch:'sil',type:'w',
+ q:'Explain the "cancelling errors" failure mode: why did the pre-58b7e2d simulator make SIL tests PASS on firmware that decoded ADC codes with the wrong reference constant?',
+ c:[{l:'sim encoded with hardcoded 3.3 regardless of selection',t:['3.3','hardcoded','encode','ignored the reference','regardless']},
+    {l:'buggy firmware decoded with the same 3.3',t:['decode','raw/4095','firmware','same constant','×3.3','* 3.3']},
+    {l:'errors cancelled → round-trip looked correct → test passed',t:['cancel','round-trip','round trip','passed','same direction','agree']},
+    {l:'fix breaks the cancellation → mis-scaling now caught',t:['fix','caught','fails','breaks','now fail','expose']}],
+ m:`The round-trip was: simulator encodes N = int(Vin/3.3 × 4095); firmware decodes V = N/4095 × 3.3. If firmware wrongly assumed 3.3 V while having selected the 2.5-V reference, the simulator — which ALSO ignored the selection and used 3.3 — encoded with the same wrong constant. The two errors cancelled exactly: V came back ≈ Vin, assertions passed, and the reference-scaling bug shipped to hardware where the real ADC encodes with 2.5 V.
+A simulator defect that agrees with a firmware defect is worse than a missing feature: it actively certifies broken code. After 58b7e2d the simulator encodes with the SELECTED reference (2.5), the firmware decodes with 3.3, and the recovered voltage is off by 3.3/2.5 = 1.32× — the SIL assertion fails, catching the bug in simulation where it belongs.`
+},
+
+{id:'s2',ch:'sil',type:'mc',
+ q:'Spot the bug in this firmware under SIL test. The simulated MSP430 has ADC:REFERENCE VREF2.5 selected and 1.5 V injected. What does read_volts() return?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm">/* firmware under test */</span>
+<span class="kw">float</span> <span class="fn">read_volts</span>(<span class="kw">void</span>) {
+    uint16_t raw = <span class="fn">adc_read_raw</span>(CH0);      <span class="cm">/* 12-bit, ref = internal 2.5 V */</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> raw / <span class="num">4095.0f</span> * <span class="num">3.3f</span>;           <span class="cm">/* convert to volts */</span>
+}`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'≈1.98 V — raw is 2457 (encoded against 2.5 V) but decoded with 3.3; the constant must match the selected reference',c:true, w:'Correct. Simulator: int(1.5/2.5 × 4095) = 2457. Firmware: 2457/4095 × 3.3 = 1.98 V — off by the ratio 3.3/2.5. The fix: multiply by 2.5f (or better, by a constant tied to the reference-selection code). Before 58b7e2d this bug was invisible because the simulator also used 3.3.'},
+  {t:'≈1.50 V — the decode constant doesn\'t matter as long as raw is correct',c:false,w:'The decode constant IS the unit of measure. 2457/4095 × 3.3 = 1.98 V, not 1.5 V. Only decoding with 2.5 recovers the injected voltage.'},
+  {t:'≈1.21 V — raw is 1861 because the simulator still encodes against 3.3 V',c:false,w:'int(1.5/3.3×4095) = 1861 was the OLD encoding. After 58b7e2d the simulator honours VREF2.5, so raw = 2457.'},
+  {t:'The code is correct — 3.3 is the required constant for a 3.3-V supply part',c:false,w:'The supply is irrelevant to decoding; the selected REFERENCE defines the code\'s unit. With VREF2.5 selected, 3.3f is simply the wrong constant.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'s3',ch:'sil',type:'w',
+ q:'Write the SCPI sequence for a SIL test that verifies brown-out rescaling: prove that AVCC-referenced conversions track a supply sag from 3.3 V to 3.0 V with a fixed 1.2-V input. Give the expected codes.',
+ c:[{l:'ADC:REFERENCE AVCC + inject 1.2',t:['reference avcc','inject 1.2','inject']},
+    {l:'at VCC 3.3 → 1489',t:['1489']},
+    {l:'CPU:VCC 3.0 then re-read → 1638',t:['cpu:vcc 3.0','1638','vcc 3.0']},
+    {l:'works because clock.vcc is read live per conversion',t:['live','per conversion','clock.vcc','no re-arm','immediately']}],
+ m:`ADC:REFERENCE AVCC          # unit of measure = the live supply rail
+ADC:CHANNEL0:INJECT 1.2     # fixed input
+ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW?           # expect int(1.2/3.3 × 4095) = 1489
+CPU:VCC 3.0                 # simulate the brown-out / battery sag
+ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW?           # expect int(1.2/3.0 × 4095) = 1638
+The second read changes with NO re-arming or re-selection because _adc_reference_voltage() reads self.clock.vcc at every conversion — AVCC is live, not a snapshot. The code RISES as the supply FALLS (shorter ruler, higher tick mark), which is exactly the inverse-transfer-function trick supply-monitoring firmware uses: measure a known voltage, infer the reference.`
+},
+
+{id:'s4',ch:'sil',type:'mc',
+ q:'After 58b7e2d, ADC:CONVERT scales by the selected reference — yet the audit still marks it NON_COMPLIANT. Why?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'It still runs without the ADC12ENC enable gate and returns \'0\' for nonexistent channels instead of raising an error',c:true, w:'Correct. The reference fix addressed scaling only. The audit entry lists the remaining defects: conversions succeed without ADC12ENC (so trigger/enable ordering bugs stay invisible) and an invalid channel yields \'0\' rather than an error (probe: adc_convert_invalid_channel).'},
+  {t:'It still divides by the hardcoded 3.3 V',c:false,w:'No — 58b7e2d fixed _adc_convert too: it now calls _adc_reference_voltage(). The NON_COMPLIANT verdict is for enable/error-handling behaviour.'},
+  {t:'It only works for channel 0',c:false,w:'It works for any existing channel; the defect is that NONEXISTENT channels return \'0\' silently.'},
+  {t:'The audit was not regenerated after the fix',c:false,w:'It was — the commit updates the report (19.8% → 22.8%) and the ADC:CONVERT row\'s wording to "Now scales by the selected reference like CHANNELn:RAW, but still…".'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'s5',ch:'sil',type:'w',
+ q:'A regression test asserts int(process("ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW?")) == 1489 after injecting 1.2 V, and it started failing with 4095 after another test file gained an ADC:REFERENCE VREF line. Diagnose the failure and give two robust fixes.',
+ c:[{l:'reference state persists across tests on a shared device',t:['persist','leak','shared','state','previous test','another test']},
+    {l:'1489 assumes AVCC 3.3; VREF makes 1.2 V full scale (4095)',t:['assume','avcc','full scale','4095','1489']},
+    {l:'fix 1: select the reference explicitly in setup (or *RST)',t:['explicit','setup','*rst','select the reference','reference avcc']},
+    {l:'fix 2: compute expected from the queried reference, not a constant',t:['comput','derive','query','function of','not a constant','parameteri']}],
+ m:`Diagnosis: the expected value 1489 silently assumes VR+ = 3.3 V (AVCC default). The reference is device-level state that persists until changed or *RST; the other test selected VREF (1.2 V), and if the device instance (or its setup) is shared, this test now converts 1.2 V against a 1.2-V reference → full scale, 4095. The test was never wrong about the ADC — it was wrong about its own preconditions.
+Fixes:
+1. Make the precondition explicit: send *RST or ADC:REFERENCE AVCC in the test's own setup so it owns every input to the transfer function. (Fresh fixtures per test achieve the same isolation.)
