+
+<p>Identical input, two references, codes of <strong>4095 vs 1489</strong>. This exact pair is the audit probe (<code>adc_ignores_reference</code>) that exposed the bug: the old model returned 1489 <em>regardless</em> of the selected reference.</p>
+
+<h3>Worked example 3 — recovering volts from a code</h3>
+<p>Firmware and test scripts invert the transfer function to get volts back:</p>
+<pre><code>V<sub>in</sub> ≈ N / FS × (V<sub>R+</sub> − V<sub>R−</sub>) + V<sub>R−</sub>
+     = 1489 / 4095 × 3.3
+     = <span class="num">1.19993 V</span>   <span class="cm"># ~1.2 V recovered — but ONLY if you divide by the SAME reference</span></code></pre>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ Truncation, not rounding</div>
+  Silmulator's nominal profile uses <code>int(voltage / full_scale_v * max_code)</code> — Python's <code>int()</code> truncates toward zero. An ideal result of 2047.5 becomes <code>2047</code>, not 2048. Keep this in mind when predicting exact codes in the drill.
+</div>
+</section>
