+<section>
+<h2>7. Often Confused</h2>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Confusion</th><th>The distinction</th></tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"Code 4095 means 3.3 V"</strong></td>
+    <td>Code 4095 means <em>V<sub>in</sub> ≥ V<sub>R+</sub></em>, whatever V<sub>R+</sub> currently is. Under VREF it means ≥1.2 V; it can also mean the input is clipping far above the reference.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Reference voltage vs. supply voltage</strong></td>
+    <td>AVCC can <em>serve as</em> the reference, but they are distinct roles. The supply powers the chip; the reference defines the conversion's unit of measure. Only when <code>ADC12VRSEL</code> selects AVCC do they coincide — and then supply noise becomes measurement noise.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong><code>VREF</code> vs <code>VREF2.5</code></strong></td>
+    <td>Bare <code>VREF</code> is not "some generic reference" — it maps to REFVSEL's reset value, 00b = 1.2 V. <code>VREF1.2/2.0/2.5</code> select explicitly. Sending <code>ADC:REFERENCE VREF</code> and expecting 2.5-V scaling is a real trap.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong><code>ADC:CHANNELn:VALUE?</code> vs <code>:RAW?</code></strong></td>
+    <td><code>VALUE?</code> returns the injected voltage in volts — test-harness introspection, no real-register counterpart (UNDOCUMENTED in the audit). <code>RAW?</code> is the modelled register read: a code, scaled by the reference. Only RAW goes through the transfer function.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>-224 vs -104</strong></td>
+    <td>-224 "Illegal parameter value": right type, not in the permitted set (<code>ADC:REFERENCE BANANA</code>). -104 "Data type error": wrong type entirely (<code>ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,notanumber</code> — the voltage must parse as a float).</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Full-scale <em>code</em> vs full-scale <em>voltage</em></strong></td>
+    <td>The full-scale code is fixed by resolution (4095 at 12-bit). The full-scale voltage is whatever reference you selected. Changing the reference moves the voltage that produces 4095; it never changes 4095 itself.</td>
+  </tr>
+</table>
+</section>
