+    <dd>"For an absolute battery-voltage reading, don't reference AVCC — take REF_A's 2.5-V output so supply sag can't skew the measurement."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: AVCC as a reference</dt>
+    <dd>REF_A's outputs are fixed absolute voltages, immune to supply drift; AVCC tracks the supply (Silmulator reads <code>clock.vcc</code> live). Absolute measurements want REF_A; ratiometric sensors powered from the rail want AVCC.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="veref">
+  <h2>VeREF+</h2>
+  <div class="pron">SLASE54D Table 7-1 — external reference pin</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>The MSP430 pin through which a board supplies its own external positive reference — typically a precision reference IC (2.048 V, 4.096 V, …). Its voltage is a board-design decision, not a chip property, and it has no defined reset state after BOR.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>Commit 58b7e2d added <code>self._veref_v</code> (default 1.2, restored by <code>*RST</code>) and the two-parameter SCPI form <code>ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.1</code> — select the pin <em>and</em> declare what the board wires to it. A non-numeric voltage raises <code>ScpiError(-104, …)</code>, pinned by <code>test_veref_voltage_param_must_be_numeric</code>.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"Our flight board has a 2.048-V reference IC on VeREF+, so the SIL setup sends <code>ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.048</code> before any conversion."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: VREF (internal)</dt>
+    <dd>VREF tokens select REF_A's <em>on-chip</em> outputs — fixed at 1.2/2.0/2.5 V, no configuration needed. VeREF+ is <em>off-chip</em>: any voltage the board provides, which is why it is the only reference whose value the test must declare (and the only one with a -104 failure mode).</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<div class="term" id="ratiometric">
+  <h2>Ratiometric measurement</h2>
+  <div class="pron">when the supply cancels out</div>
+  <dl>
+    <dt>Definition</dt>
+    <dd>A measurement arrangement where the sensor's output and the ADC's reference derive from the same source, so the conversion measures the sensor's <em>fraction</em> and the source's absolute value cancels out of the transfer function: N = FS × (k·AVCC)/AVCC = FS × k.</dd>
+    <dt class="ex">Project example</dt>
+    <dd>Selecting <code>ADC:REFERENCE AVCC</code> makes <code>_adc_reference_voltage()</code> return <code>self.clock.vcc</code> live. A divider-style sensor modelled by injecting <code>k × vcc</code> reads the same code before and after <code>CPU:VCC 3.0</code> — while an absolute injection like the fixed 1.2 V shifts from 1489 to 1638, which is how a SIL test tells the two arrangements apart.</dd>
+    <dt class="use">Usage</dt>
+    <dd>"The potentiometer is powered from the rail, so keep the conversion ratiometric — referencing it to VREF2.5 would re-introduce the supply drift you're trying to cancel."</dd>
+    <dt class="vs">Contrast with: absolute measurement</dt>
+    <dd>Absolute measurement answers "how many volts?" and needs a supply-independent reference (REF_A or VeREF+). Ratiometric measurement answers "what fraction of the excitation?" and <em>wants</em> the supply as the reference. Choosing the wrong mode for the sensor type is a real firmware bug class the reference-aware model can now surface.</dd>
+  </dl>
+</div>
